# TARGET ARCHITECTURE: aarch64 (ARM64) | v0.19 | aws/ubuntu2404
# Enterprise Linux Audit Policy v0.19 | Tier 2 — mandatory auditd-only configuration integrity
# Source: enterprise SOC/IR CVE-independent system integrity requirement; Linux Audit auditctl manual.
# ACTIVE recursive write/attribute monitoring for /etc; covers existing files and newly created names.
# Includes loader preload creation, OpenSSH/sudoers (if installed), system profiles and sysctl configuration.
# No auid/uid filter: this includes both interactive users and services with auid=unset.
# Monitor only wa, not reads; filesystem nested mounts/bind mounts require separate suitability assessment.
# This expands volume beyond v0.18: evaluate audit event rate, audit lost/backlog and overlap with existing rules.
# Tier 1 -f 2 stays mandatory; a platform unable to support Tier 2 requires documented waiver.
# x86_64: native b64 plus compat b32; ARM64: native b64 only.
# v0.19 ACTIVE — auditd-only recursive directory integrity:
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F dir=/etc -F perm=wa -k t2_etc_integrity

# v0.17 ACTIVE: CVE-independent system-wide shell startup integrity.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/etc/profile -F perm=wa -k t2_shell_init
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/etc/bash.bashrc -F perm=wa -k t2_shell_init
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F dir=/etc/profile.d -F perm=wa -k t2_shell_init
# These rule keys are distinct from STIG watches; do not duplicate existing profile rules in other files.
# /etc/profile.d is a documented default family directory; customized/minimal images still need a path preflight.

# Magic SysRq: persistent /etc/sysctl* configuration writes are monitored above,
# but privileged writes to procfs trigger/control paths are a DIFFERENT behavior.
# Kernel-specific procfs watch acceptance is unverified offline; do not activate universally.
# In particular, kernel.sysrq=0 does not prevent privileged writes to /proc/sysrq-trigger.
# CONDITIONAL auditd-only candidates; enable only when the exact kernel accepts them:
# -a always,exit -F path=/proc/sysrq-trigger -F perm=w -k t2_sysrq
# -a always,exit -F path=/proc/sys/kernel/sysrq -F perm=wa -k t2_sysrq
# Until accepted and independently exercised, direct runtime SysRq attribution is NOT guaranteed.
# The superseded single-file loader preload proposal was removed: parent auditing covers initial creation.
